abla_i \psi}{\psi}ight)\). Here, \(\mathbf{Q}_i\) represents the position of the \(i\)-th particle, \(m_i\) is its mass, and \(\hbar\) is the reduced Planck constant. The term \(\operatorname{Im}\left( rac{
abla_i \psi}{\psi}ight)\) is the imaginary part of the gradient of the logarithm of the wave function, which is proportional to the gradient of the phase of the wave function. This means the particle's velocity is directly related to how the phase of the wave function changes in space. The second part of the equation, \(Q = -\sum_{i}  rac{\hbar^2}{2m_i}  rac{
abla_i^2 R}{R}\), defines the quantum potential \(Q\). If we write the wave function in polar form as \(\psi = R e^{iS/\hbar}\), where \(R\) is the amplitude and \(S\) is the phase, then the velocity can be expressed as \(\mathbf{v}_i =  rac{
abla_i S}{m_i}\). The quantum potential is derived from the kinetic energy term in the Schrödinger equation and acts as an effective potential that influences particle trajectories. It depends on the second spatial derivatives of the amplitude of the wave function, \(R\), and introduces non-local effects into the dynamics.

